WEBVTT

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:07.720
The history of Scandinavia and its people is written in the sea.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:18.220
It might be Einar, Knut, or Harald, names that seem to spring from Norse mythology.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.770
Their ancestors had names like Erik the Red and Life the Lucky.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:30.000
The defiant prows of Viking boats are their link to the past and the present.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:36.870
Ten centuries ago, their fierce ancestors rode the oceans as pirates and plunderers.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:45.240
They come to harvest its riches and perhaps to satisfy some ongoing spirit of adventure and excitement.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.070
Modern Viking is still on the sea.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:30.000
Mike Farrell. Welcome to the best of the National Geographic specials. With each of these programs in

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.270
highly acclaimed series from the National Geographic Society, we embark on a richly

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:39.640
rewarding adventure. Explore with us man's continuing quest for our world's hidden wonders,

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:45.370
its enduring beauty, and drama. Like the best of adventures, our journey of discovery ultimately

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:51.590
takes us to a deeper understanding of ourselves. Vikings. The word alone was enough to conjure up

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:55.900
terror throughout Europe when those seafaring warriors pillaged the continent, but that was

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.570
a thousand years ago. The spirit of the Norseman has long departed.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.470
or has it barbarian invaders no longer,

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:07.570
the descendants of the Vikings still retain the fierce legacy of their ancestors,

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.770
channeled now toward a different sort of conquest.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.870
Here are the Scandinavian fishermen, the last Vikings,

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:23.720
triumphing in their battle against the sea.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:29.540
Above the Arctic Circle along Norway's northwest coast,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.800
Lofoten Islands. With mountains still unscarred by industry and waterways free

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.260
of modern commerce, the Lofotens are a vision of what northern Scandinavia's

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:56.550
seacoast once was like. In the beginning, the people clinging to the northern

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.000
coasts and fjords were primitive and isolated fishing tribes.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.600
Some have always remained peaceful and contented fishermen.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.290
In late winter, the waters are thick with spawning cod.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.340
Since the Stone Age, men have clustered along the rugged shores and looked to the sea for survival.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.320
From the beginning, fishing has been the basis of their life.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:36.440
On these northern islands, the brief summer is not a holiday, but a time to repair the

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:47.670
scars of an Arctic winter.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:51.910
And it is a time to prepare the winter catch for shipment to nearby Sweden and as far away

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:52.910
as Nigeria.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:04:00.000
Through the endless days of the midnight sun, the cod continues to dry until all the moisture

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:14.680
out and the fish is hard as rock hundreds of years the cod has been prepared this way

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:24.680
once it would have been carried to spain and italy in wooden sailing ships out of such fjords and

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:30.000
harbors shrouded by mist and mountains out of norway sweden and denmark and

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.220
came the great seafarers of the North,

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:35.750
the men the world remembers as the Vikings.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.850
By the 8th century, people of the North had become masters of the sea,

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.930
and it was their gateway to the world.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:58.450
Unknown generations ago, the Viking ship evolved,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.000
shaped by artisans,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.280
who passed their skills from sun to sun.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.750
Without blueprint, with eye and memory as their only reference,

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.520
they still build boats with unmistakable Viking lines.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.310
To the Vikings and their descendants,

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:22.730
a boat has always been a work of art,

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.400
an expression of its creator, a piece of sculpture.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:39.120
The present-day boatbuilder still follows the same design, used to reconstruct a thousand-year-old relic.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:47.480
The ship they reassemble was sunk by Vikings to protect the Danish town of Roeschilde from enemy fleets.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:53.480
Scandinavian archaeologists and historians are not only rebuilding ancient structures,

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:58.480
they are piecing together a true picture of the way their Norse ancestors lived.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:09.350
Constructed along the lines of a ship, a reproduced barracks at Trelleborg, Denmark, stands by the site of a massive Viking fortress.

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:20.100
Ringed by an earthen rampart 55 feet thick, the military encampment was built about the year 1000.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:30.000
Inside the boat-shaped barracks, 1,200 Viking warriors honed their spears, mended shields, and prepared a well-planned

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:49.680
invasion of England capture their Viking heritage a group of Danes built an

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:59.000
authentic Viking ship using Viking tools and traditional designs

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.940
And so across the North Sea, their ancestors came,

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:08.720
terrorizing the known world for two and a half centuries.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.570
Triggered by a population explosion and internal strife,

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.870
men of Norway, Sweden and Denmark

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:18.170
swarmed out of Scandinavia in quest of land and wealth.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.840
In their language, Viking meant piracy and plunder,

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.430
and they became the Vikings.

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:30.000
From the British Isles to Russia,

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:40.940
Europe prayed, "From the fury of the Northmen, deliver us, O Lord."

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:45.790
Across the northern seas, they pushed westward to Iceland, and Greenland, the world's largest

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:51.720
island, colonized by Eric the Red.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.150
