alcohol ever Engvall dude would not tolerate it and

00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:21.630
none would dare oppose him younger men now rule the family's fortunes on the

00:29:21.630 --> 00:29:26.710
open sea but at this table the older generations are still held in great

00:29:26.710 --> 00:29:30.000
esteem what is they who have passed on to their son

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:32.660
the life inherited from their fathers.

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:35.340
These family gatherings are rare,

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:38.310
for the men spend most of their lives at sea.

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:51.650
500 years on some small boat in the North Atlantic,

00:29:52.370 --> 00:29:54.690
men named Dib have lived and worked together,

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:57.270
day after day, year after year,

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:00.000
isolated on their own tiny Bobby Browns.

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:08.760
Now there are 10 of these men named Dib, still facing the sea, their ancient enemy, and their only provider.

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:23.990
350 miles from Norway's coast and midway to Iceland, the men of the Dib family prepare the lines and bait the hooks.

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:28.760
The Leicestershire begins to prowl the North Atlantic in search of cod.

00:30:33.420 --> 00:30:39.540
A thousand years ago, their Viking ancestors crossed this angry sea to Iceland, navigating

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:42.390
by the sun and the stars.

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:49.680
Now they use electronics, not only to maintain their course, but to pursue their prey.

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:56.270
The echo sounder helps detect the schools of fish, and the technology of man is verified

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:58.810
by nature's timeless signal.

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:00.000
Hungry gulps from the nearby forest.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:09.940
Faroe Islands. At the beginning of the run, a buoy is dropped, weighted with a

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:19.310
small anchor. The Leicestershire does not use a trawl, but feeds out hundreds of

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:26.700
yards of baited line marked by floating buoys. Below decks, the line with hooks

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:30.000
every few feet is carefully played out, while the

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:35.700
maintains its course and speed.

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:38.510
By buoys and dragged down by anchors,

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:40.470
the line settles on the bottom

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:42.070
while the ship moves on to lay out

00:31:42.070 --> 00:31:43.610
other runs of baited line.

00:31:57.740 --> 00:32:00.000
After finishing the final run, the first lines

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:03.190
which may have been out for hours, are finally hauled in.

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:08.590
The first deck team is Harold Dube and Asbjorn Dube, father and son.

00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:12.790
This is the life of the Leicestershire's men,

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:15.800
out to sea for two or three weeks at a time,

00:32:16.350 --> 00:32:18.010
three or four hours of sleep a night,

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:21.880
back to Olison to unload, then home to Dube for a while.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:27.000
For most of the year, they fish in winter waters that are stormy and unpredictable.

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:30.000
And in the summer, they are often plagued by fog.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:37.590
This is their life, winter and summer, from youth to old age.

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:41.320
Without a waste of motion, this operation will go on for hours

00:32:41.320 --> 00:32:45.160
as one man replaces another.

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:48.810
Like some strange relay team in a fisherman's Olympics,

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:52.250
they race against time to clear the hooks of valuable cod

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:56.140
and worthless skate, to rebate the lines

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:58.660
and to rush them over the sides again.

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:00.000
The sooner the hold is filled,

00:33:00.410 --> 00:33:13.950
sooner they head for home. Through the weeks on a desolate sea, the men regard the gulls as

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:25.040
companions, other living creatures who depend upon the fish for life. In the midst of the

00:33:25.040 --> 00:33:30.000
fishing run, Andreas Dube, the ship's engineer, becomes worried by a persistent

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:33.180
knocking in the engine

00:33:33.460 --> 00:33:36.080
the speed of the ship is drastically reduced

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:49.330
captain envied is told by andreas his uncle that the engine must be checked

00:33:49.330 --> 00:33:54.130
a task which cannot be carried out at sea

00:33:55.410 --> 00:33:58.610
they decide to run for port in the faroe islands

00:34:03.450 --> 00:34:09.680
Two days fishing lost the Leicestershire hoists the Faroe Islands flag and heads for Torshaven harbor

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:23.290
Intentionally these modern Norsemen have retraced one of the earliest voyages of their forefathers

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:26.110
At the turn of the 8th century

00:34:26.640 --> 00:34:30.000
Vikings from the coast of Norway first sailed into the Faroe Islands

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:40.650
Here some settled and founded the civilization which thrives today.

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:50.780
100 miles from Scandinavian shores, these rugged islands were settled by Vikings from

00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:53.280
the coast of Norway.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:58.210
Now nominally a part of Denmark, the men of the pharaohs have their own language, flag,

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:00.000
and currency.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:15.430
traditions, culture, and individual lifestyle. In the ninth century, when

00:35:15.430 --> 00:35:19.940
Vikings first landed on these Atlantic islands, they found Irish hermits tending

00:35:19.940 --> 00:35:26.910
sheep. They drove out the hermits, but kept the sheep. Today the islands are

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:30.000
called the Faroes, the Sheep Islands.

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:35.410
And there are more than 70,000 of these animals, almost twice the human population.

00:35:41.050 --> 00:35:46.070
These animals are vital to the islanders. Their wool clothes them.

00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:49.370
Their meat, fresh, salted or dried, feeds them.

00:35:50.710 --> 00:35:55.940
In the summer, sheep are rounded up to be wormed, treated for sickness and sheared.

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:58.710
For the young, it has always been a special time.

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:22.700
years some of the young are no longer content to be sheep herders many are

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:28.260
leaving the herds the land in the past to the old

00:36:31.400 --> 00:36:38.440
In a strange way, tradition remains a part of the pharaoh's economy.

00:36:38.440 --> 00:36:44.240
In the mid-19th century, these looms provided yarn for nearly 100,000 hand-knit sweaters

00:36:44.240 --> 00:36:49.010
treasured in the fashionable shops of Europe.

00:36:49.010 --> 00:37:00.000
These people whom time has forgotten are masters of a vanishing craft.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:07.960
According to challenges centuries old, young Faroese still thrill to ancient Viking sports.

00:37:07.960 --> 00:37:13.240
As they skim across Torshavan Harbor, their traditional high-proud racing boats are symbols

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:16.020
of how little life has changed here.

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:24.260
But in the days ahead, some will make one final trip across Torshavan Harbor and leave

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:26.120
the Faroe Islands forever.

00:37:34.030 --> 00:37:40.390
generations, the sea is not a means of escape, but a source of life. With a decoy, they hunt the

00:37:40.390 --> 00:37:46.450
guillemots, the diving waterfowl which flourishes in the northern seas. When the birds try to land

00:37:46.450 --> 00:37:56.400
on the pitching well-trapped board, their feet are caught in wire snares. To these older islanders,

00:37:56.400 --> 00:37:59.510
it has never occurred that there is anywhere else to live.

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:05.220
Here you can always live off the sea and the land, off the fish and the birds.

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:20.210
The island's endless volcanic cliffs, pockmarked with crevices and lined with ledges, form a monumental nesting ground.

00:38:20.210 --> 00:38:23.110
A breeding place for species from north and south.

00:38:23.110 --> 00:38:29.590
These islands comprise one of the greatest bird concentrations in the world, over 200 species.

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:49.610
millions of wild birds. Each year, a unique variety of hunter invades the cliffs. The

00:38:49.610 --> 00:39:00.000
vertical cliffs rise up nearly 2,000 feet from the pounding surf. In their traditional mountaineering

00:39:00.030 --> 00:39:06.300
Pharaoh, the egg snatchers clamor over these treacherous cliffs for nine days, a time limit

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:14.720
set to allow the birds to lay other eggs and survive. In June, the ledges are filled with

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:20.790
millions of freshly laid eggs from the guillemots and razorbills, gannets, shearwaters, stormy

00:39:20.790 --> 00:39:27.490
petrels, and countless others. Each group of birds finds its own niche in the cliff walls.

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:42.940
still lose their lives pursuing this age-old tradition and sport.

00:39:44.700 --> 00:39:51.250
The eggs they hunt are a delicacy, a food supplement for winter when the diet is meager and unburyable.

00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:14.110
Isolated for centuries, the rugged men of the pharaohs are the direct descendants of history's legendary seafarers.

00:40:14.920 --> 00:40:18.580
Their veins still courses the blood of the Vikings.

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:26.350
They are a dramatic reminder that these towering isles of rock have created a uniquely independent breed of men.

00:40:47.150 --> 00:40:52.550
Touched by the outside world, a way of life has survived beyond its time.

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:59.670
These rugged northern islands are our last remaining outpost of the Viking spirit.

00:41:11.450 --> 00:41:13.330
has shaped Scandinavia's character

00:41:13.330 --> 00:41:15.430
and history. A Norwegian

00:41:15.430 --> 00:41:17.580
once said, what would we be

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:19.580
without the sea? A handful

00:41:19.580 --> 00:41:21.180
of people on a pile of rock.

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:23.960
It is the sea that unites us.

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:28.080
Even today, the young people

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:30.000
of Scandinavia are never far

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:34.060
from the smell of the sea or from its memories.

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:36.300
These Norwegian children read the great sagas

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:38.200
of the Norsemen.

00:41:38.200 --> 00:41:41.060
They know of Viking conquests, of explorers

00:41:41.060 --> 00:41:43.850
and navigators and fishermen.

00:41:43.850 --> 00:41:46.410
Many of their parents and grandparents

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:48.550
still live off the sea.

00:41:48.550 --> 00:41:50.790
And 80% of Norway's people are clustered

00:41:50.790 --> 00:41:55.520
in towns and villages along a 2,000 mile coastline.

00:41:55.520 --> 00:42:00.000
Yet for the young, the future holds other promises in a world

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:01.480
has already changed.

00:42:03.540 --> 00:42:10.480
As long as there are ships in the harbors

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:11.700
and oceans of the world,

00:42:12.370 --> 00:42:13.950
Scandinavians will be manning them.

00:42:14.430 --> 00:42:16.470
For the destiny of many will always

00:42:16.470 --> 00:42:17.490
lie with the sea.

00:42:20.680 --> 00:42:26.020
Flags of the modern Norsemen proclaim

00:42:26.020 --> 00:42:27.970
a continuing mastery of the oceans

00:42:27.970 --> 00:42:30.000
and symbolize a way of

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.740
life that has lasted for over a thousand years.

00:42:33.910 --> 00:42:43.360
The Norsemen no longer come as conquerors.

00:42:44.210 --> 00:42:50.460
The warlike Vikings have passed into history, but to their descendants, they bequeath the

00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:55.020
legacy that time cannot seem to change, a love for the sea.

00:42:55.020 --> 00:42:55.860
A love for the sea.

00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:06.080
Those intrepid descendants of the Vikings who now inhabit the Faroe Islands

00:43:06.080 --> 00:43:10.130
and the waters between Iceland and Great Britain have prospered in a rugged environment.

00:43:10.990 --> 00:43:14.350
The government-supported fishing industries played a significant role in that prosperity.

00:43:15.220 --> 00:43:18.660
The growing population of the Faroe Islands, 46,000 at last count,

00:43:19.120 --> 00:43:21.740
enjoys one of the highest per capita incomes in all of Europe.

00:43:22.960 --> 00:43:25.640
Next time, on the best of the National Geographic specials,

00:43:25.640 --> 00:43:30.000
we'll explore another corner of the endlessly rewarding and surprising world around us.

00:43:30.440 --> 00:43:33.190
I'm Mike Farrell for the National Geographic Society.

